=======================================================================
0. ABSOLUTE ANTI-HALLUCINATION CONTRACT
=======================================================================

These rules override every other instruction in this document.

0.1. NO FAKE IMPLEMENTATION

Never ship a production path that merely returns a successful-looking result
without performing the requested operation.

Forbidden examples:
- mockEngine in the production execution path;
- fake tool success;
- fabricated command output;
- hardcoded model catalogues presented as live;
- generated token streams presented as actual inference;
- simulated file mutations presented as real writes;
- fake test counts;
- UI buttons without real handlers;
- "connected" status without a live connection;
- fallback behavior that quietly executes a different operation;
- empty handlers that return success;
- TODO implementations advertised as available.

A feature is either IMPLEMENTED, PARTIAL, UNAVAILABLE, BLOCKED, or UNVERIFIED.
Its UI, diagnostics, documentation, and tests must reflect that status.

Demo fixtures may exist only behind an explicit opt-in flag such as
CHAKKI_DEMO=1. Every demo screen must visibly say DEMO. Demo fixtures must
never be loaded automatically in a production session.

If the provider is unavailable, return a typed provider-unavailable result.
Preserve the user's data and show a clear status. Do not impersonate an LLM
with a deterministic script and pretend that autonomous reasoning happened.

0.2. NO FALSE TESTS

A test is not valid merely because it is green.

Every test must:
- execute the behavior it claims to test;
- assert an observable contract;
- fail when the relevant behavior is deliberately broken;
- use a fresh binary built from the current source;
- separate setup/build failures from assertion failures and process crashes;
- avoid assertions that are trivially true by construction;
- avoid random expectations that vary between runs;
- preserve the original working tree during destructive/mutation tests.

Mocks are permitted at explicitly defined external boundaries, such as the
HTTP transport used by offline provider tests. They must not replace the
subsystem whose real behavior is being tested.

No live provider request is required by normal offline verification.

0.3. NO INFINITE CONTEXT MARKETING

The language model has a finite context window. Never claim that all prior
messages are simultaneously active inside that window.

The product promise is:

CANONICAL HISTORY IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY LOST.
RELEVANT HISTORY CAN BE RETRIEVED WITH PROVENANCE.
RETRIEVAL FAILURE IS VISIBLE AND TESTABLE.
DERIVED MEMORY NEVER REPLACES THE ORIGINAL RECORD.

0.4. NO UNVERIFIED PLATFORM CLAIMS

Do not claim Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, iOS, or browser support merely
because code contains a conditional compilation branch or build config.

Record the environment and actual command used to establish support.
An unavailable compiler or signing environment is a blocker, not a pass.

0.5. NO REWRITING FOR THE SAKE OF REWRITING

Inspect the actual repository first.
Preserve useful existing work.
Make changes incrementally.
Do not overwrite or delete unrelated user files.
Do not create a second product tree if a viable implementation already
exists in the current workspace.

