7. MEMORY: CANONICAL LEDGER, NO AUTOMATIC HISTORY LOSS
=======================================================================

This is a flagship requirement, not a cosmetic feature.

7.1 CANONICAL STORAGE

Each conversation has an append-only event ledger.

A ledger event contains at least:
- schema version;
- session ID;
- event sequence;
- event ID;
- event kind and role;
- timestamp;
- exact content or an immutable content reference;
- payload length;
- previous-event hash;
- current-event SHA-256;
- source/provenance metadata where applicable.

Use segmented JSONL or a similarly inspectable append format.
Target segment size: 8 MiB maximum payload plus bounded metadata overhead.
Rotate a segment before it exceeds its defined bound.

Writes are serialized and durable according to a documented fsync policy.
A valid record is committed before its associated side effect is executed.
Sensitive credentials are never included in the ledger.

The hash chain detects modifications relative to a trusted recorded head;
do not advertise it as protection against a privileged attacker who can
rewrite both the ledger and all checkpoints.

Recovery must detect:
- truncated final record;
- malformed records;
- sequence gaps;
- hash-chain mismatch;
- missing/corrupt segments;
- missing index data.

Never silently skip a damaged record or claim successful recovery if
validation fails. Preserve damaged bytes and use a recovery/continuation
strategy that does not rewrite immutable prior segments.

7.2 NO AUTOMATIC LOSSY COMPACTION

Never replace canonical history with a summary.

No automatic:
- summary replacing original messages;
- truncation of canonical messages;
- deletion of an earlier turn to remain under a context limit;
- silent removal of tool results;
- rewrite of old ledger records;
- shrinking the canonical history database to fit a model window.

Derived indexes, typed facts and task links are allowed because they do
not replace the source records.

Explicit user-requested deletion is a separate data-governance operation
with confirmation, clear semantics and tested removal from active storage.
Do not secretly interpret an explicit delete request as a mere UI hide.

7.3 LAST-100 WINDOW

The main chat efficiently renders the latest 100 user-facing messages.

This is a presentation window, NOT the retention boundary.

The 101st message moves the oldest message out of the default viewport
only. The original record and all its associated events remain durable
and addressable.

Provide:
- virtualized rendering;
- historical timeline navigation;
- jump to exact turn number;
- search by exact text and identifiers;
- retrieve a specific message by stable ID/sequence;
- show complete tool inputs/outputs for a selected event when authorized;
- show provenance from retrieved memory back to the original event.

A user must be able to ask:
"What did we discuss in message 34?"
and receive the exact relevant source context for the model.

A user must also be able to ask:
"What remains unfinished from the recommendation in message 107?"
The retrieval path must load that exact message and any durable tasks/actions
linked to its items. The agent must compare those obligations with actual
task/run/test records, not hallucinate a completion status.

Preserve explicit message/turn numbering rules. A turn may contain multiple
events; do not confuse turn number with event sequence.

7.4 RETRIEVAL — NO EMBEDDINGS

Use this deterministic local retrieval baseline:

A. Exact event, turn and message lookup.
B. SQLite FTS5/BM25 full-text index for user/assistant/tool records.
C. Exact identifier index for paths, versions, model IDs, URLs, commit SHAs,
   error codes, symbols, function names and other structured identifiers.
D. Structured decision/task/fact records linked to immutable source records.
E. Recency and task relevance as explicit ranking features, not a replacement
   for provenance.
F. Optional deterministic query normalization/tokenization.

Do not add embeddings, a vector database, a required local semantic model,
or a mandatory query-expansion LLM call.

FTS5 and exact identifiers are the default strategy. If paraphrased retrieval
does not find a fact, return a truthful low-confidence/no-result status;
do not claim semantic understanding that was never implemented.

A retrieval result must include:
- source session;
- turn/event ID;
- original sequence;
- content excerpt;
- ranking reasons or retrieval method;
- confidence/rank metadata where meaningful;
- a path to load the complete original content.

Never expose another session's memory through indexing or ranking.

7.5 TASK CONTINUITY

Persist explicit task/action records separately from ordinary free-form
conversation text.

Each action may include:
- action ID;
- source message/event;
- source item ordinal;
- title and exact source text;
- creation time;
- state (open, in progress, blocked, done, cancelled);
- dependencies;
- implementing run/commit/test references;
- verification evidence;
- latest status change.

Do not claim an item is done from a model's assertion alone. Completion
requires a linked action or verification event.

Assistant messages with numbered recommendations remain immutable. When
a user references a prior recommendation, load the referenced message and
the corresponding action status rather than relying on a lossy summary.

If arbitrary free-form advice has not been converted into structured action
items, retrieve the complete source message and acknowledge any remaining
uncertainty. Do not invent a task ledger entry retroactively as if it had
always existed.

7.6 MEMORY FAILURE AND RETENTION

A failed index must not destroy conversation history.

Provide safe index rebuild and report its state:
healthy
rebuilding
degraded
failed

A missing/corrupt index may be rebuilt from the canonical ledger.
Index data is derived and disposable; source history is not.

No retention sweep or deletion may run silently. Document storage locations,
export, explicit deletion and recovery behavior.

7.7 REQUIRED MEMORY BENCHMARK

Build a deterministic offline benchmark with:
- at least 100,000 events;
- an early exact fact and its complete original bytes;
- message-number lookup;
- relevant retrieval among distractors;
- correction/supersession;
- cross-session isolation;
- tool-result recovery;
- source provenance;
- restart/rebuild;
- corrupted index recovery;
- damaged ledger diagnostics;
- context budget overflow;
- explicit deletion behavior.

Measure:
- append throughput;
- index build/update time;
- exact lookup latency;
- FTS/BM25 retrieval latency;
- session reopen time;
- replay/recovery time;
- memory/RSS usage;
- ledger size;
- bytes preserved;
- retrieval precision and recall on a fixed test corpus;
- cross-session leakage count.

Benchmark the real implementation, not an isolated toy structure.

Define record sizes, hardware, cold/warm state and sample count. Target
reopening a healthy 100k-event session within 1 second on the documented
reference machine, then report whether this was achieved.

Ledger size must equal the stored source payload plus declared framing,
metadata and integrity overhead, not an unsupported claim of zero overhead.

