10. PATCH TRANSACTION AND APPROVAL ENGINE
=======================================================================

File editing is a first-class transaction.

For proposed filesystem edits:
1. Read the actual current file.
2. Capture a pre-change hash/version.
3. Generate a structured patch.
4. Validate syntax and path policy.
5. Produce a real unified diff.
6. Persist a proposal record and its artifacts.
7. Wait for the applicable approval policy.
8. Before applying, recheck preconditions so stale diffs cannot overwrite
   another actor's newer changes.
9. Apply the patch through a staged transaction.
10. Validate the resulting file and expected postconditions.
11. Persist the actual result and file hashes.
12. Notify all authorized clients.

Default policy:
- read-only operations are allowed in the workspace;
- writes, deletes and shell execution require approval;
- high-risk and external side effects require explicit approval.

Offer configurable permission profiles:
- Review: ask for mutating operations;
- Workspace: pre-authorize selected edits and build/test commands inside
  the chosen workspace;
- Autopilot: allow scoped, reversible work within a pre-approved workspace
  and tool policy.

Autopilot is not unrestricted shell access.

Even in Autopilot, never silently grant:
- access outside the workspace;
- secret/keychain/credential reads;
- arbitrary external network access;
- publication/push/release actions;
- mass deletion;
- destructive system-level commands;
- privilege escalation;
- modifications to unrelated repositories;
- actions outside the configured red-team target.

A red-team mode may perform authorized security testing inside an explicitly
defined scope. It must not become a general permission bypass.

Tool state machine:

pending
  -> approved -> running -> completed
  -> rejected
  -> cancelled
  -> expired
  -> failed

Cancellation during execution:
- cancel the real process;
- terminate its process group when applicable;
- wait for termination with a bounded timeout;
- use escalation only when necessary;
- report partial effects accurately;
- recover staged file transactions where the engine has sufficient before
  images and transaction metadata.

Do not claim that a killed shell command was rolled back if it performed
external or irreversible actions.

Multi-file changes require a recoverable transaction journal. If a crash
occurs between files, startup recovery must identify incomplete transactions
and either finish or restore them according to verified preconditions.

The UI must not show a green success state until the real tool outcome has
been received and checked.

