=======================================================================

The product must include an actual orchestrator, not a prompt that simply
asks the same model to pretend to be several agents.

DIRECTOR responsibilities:
- inspect task requirements;
- establish a task graph;
- choose specialized roles;
- dispatch work;
- monitor state;
- resolve dependencies and conflicts;
- enforce budgets and permissions;
- request verification;
- aggregate evidence and report the outcome.

The Director does not directly mutate project files. It delegates execution
through the same controlled task/tool interfaces. It may read task metadata
and results needed for orchestration.

At minimum implement two real specialized subagents:

1. EXPLORER
   - read-only by default;
   - inspects the repository;
   - maps relevant code/dependencies/tests;
   - returns file/symbol references and evidence;
   - cannot modify project files.

2. WORKER
   - receives a bounded task and explicit write scope;
   - implements one assigned change;
   - reports files changed, tests and residual risks;
   - cannot expand its write scope on its own.

Add an independently operating VERIFIER role:
- inspect results;
- run relevant checks;
- verify claims against code and output;
- reject unsupported PASS reports.

The system may allocate 5–10 workers to tasks that genuinely benefit from
parallelism, within configurable resource/provider limits. It must not
spawn ten agents for a one-file fix merely to look autonomous.

Parallel workers must not edit the same working tree blindly.
Use separate worktrees or another enforceable conflict-isolation strategy
for independent write tasks. All integration goes through reviewed diffs.

Agent tasks have:
- task ID;
- parent task ID;
- explicit objective;
- read/write scope;
- inputs;
- expected deliverables;
- budget and timeout;
- current state;
- artifacts and provenance;
- verification criteria;
- termination reason.

Enforce:
- cancellation;
- per-agent concurrency limits;
- time/budget ceilings;
- cycle detection in task dependencies;
- no infinite orchestration loops;
- retry limits;
- deadlock detection;
- bounded provider rate-limit handling;
- recovery after agent/core failure.

Do not claim subagent parallelism unless independent tasks actually ran
concurrently.

